WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:02.923
- Sorry Goose, but it's
time to buzz the tower.

00:00:02.923 --> 00:00:06.090
(jet engine whirring)

00:00:07.360 --> 00:00:10.280
- All right, that seems super bad.

00:00:10.280 --> 00:00:11.830
- Yeah, as Maverick's defense attorney,

00:00:11.830 --> 00:00:14.460
I've gotta say he needs to, at this point,

00:00:14.460 --> 00:00:17.430
not say anything to anyone
about what just happened,

00:00:17.430 --> 00:00:19.090
lock himself into a room

00:00:19.090 --> 00:00:20.290
and hope for the best.

00:00:20.290 --> 00:00:21.765
- We're gonna have a good time.

00:00:21.765 --> 00:00:22.750
(bottle clinks)
- Always.

00:00:22.750 --> 00:00:24.630
- There's a new "Top
Gun", which reminded me,

00:00:24.630 --> 00:00:27.090
they broke a whole bunch of
laws in the original movie,

00:00:27.090 --> 00:00:28.550
but to talk about all the military law

00:00:28.550 --> 00:00:29.920
that Maverick and Goose broke,

00:00:29.920 --> 00:00:30.760
I needed some help.

00:00:30.760 --> 00:00:33.090
So I called in some
air support, my friend,

00:00:33.090 --> 00:00:35.330
the lawyer and former
Major in the Marines,

00:00:35.330 --> 00:00:38.070
the scowl owl to my legal Eagle, Spencer.

00:00:38.070 --> 00:00:39.090
Spencer, thanks for coming.

00:00:39.090 --> 00:00:40.210
- Happy to be here, Devin.

00:00:40.210 --> 00:00:42.560
- So apparently everyone on the internet

00:00:42.560 --> 00:00:44.170
is an expert in military law,

00:00:44.170 --> 00:00:45.780
but what makes you qualified

00:00:45.780 --> 00:00:48.330
to talk about all of the
broken laws in "Top Gun"?

00:00:48.330 --> 00:00:49.950
- I was a judge advocate
in the Marine Corps

00:00:49.950 --> 00:00:51.170
where I prosecuted people,

00:00:51.170 --> 00:00:53.670
including on Marine
Corps Air Base, Miramar,

00:00:53.670 --> 00:00:56.010
which is where "Top Gun" was actually set.

00:00:56.010 --> 00:00:57.350
I was also an artillery officer

00:00:57.350 --> 00:00:59.070
and a joint terminal attack controller.

00:00:59.070 --> 00:01:01.300
I did that in Iraq, where I
was calling in airstrikes.

00:01:01.300 --> 00:01:03.650
And what that means is I was
working with pilots to my left,

00:01:03.650 --> 00:01:05.440
to my right and above me.

00:01:05.440 --> 00:01:07.960
And I saw the good, the bad and the ugly.

00:01:07.960 --> 00:01:09.740
And now I'm a deputy district attorney

00:01:09.740 --> 00:01:11.000
in the Pacific Northwest

00:01:11.000 --> 00:01:12.720
where I prosecute major felony crimes.

00:01:12.720 --> 00:01:14.640
- All right, internet,
a real former marine

00:01:14.640 --> 00:01:16.340
has entered the chat, so beware.

00:01:16.340 --> 00:01:18.100
- Devin, I've got the need.

00:01:18.100 --> 00:01:19.460
- The need for speed?

00:01:19.460 --> 00:01:21.565
- No, the need to put some pilots in jail.

00:01:21.565 --> 00:01:23.477
- (laughing) All right, let's dig in.

00:01:23.477 --> 00:01:24.310
(jet engine whirring)

00:01:24.310 --> 00:01:26.530
- [Pilot] Whew, he's
never (indistinct) before.

00:01:26.530 --> 00:01:27.810
- [Commander] What the
hell are they doing there?

00:01:27.810 --> 00:01:29.910
- You know, it's a minor detail,

00:01:29.910 --> 00:01:32.930
but there's no actual
such thing as a MiG-28.

00:01:32.930 --> 00:01:34.470
- Really, there's no MiG-28?

00:01:34.470 --> 00:01:35.470
That sounds like a real thing.

00:01:35.470 --> 00:01:39.400
- We happen to see a MiG-28,
do a 4G negative dive.

00:01:39.400 --> 00:01:41.380
- No, there's actually a MiG-29,

00:01:41.380 --> 00:01:42.670
but I think they just subtracted one

00:01:42.670 --> 00:01:43.543
for this film for some reason.

00:01:43.543 --> 00:01:45.210
- The MiG-28?

00:01:45.210 --> 00:01:49.540
- Oh, okay, so what are the
planes they're standing in for?

00:01:49.540 --> 00:01:51.530
Well, whatever bad guy is

00:01:52.540 --> 00:01:53.713
antagonizing the Americans here.

00:01:53.713 --> 00:01:55.230
- The MiG-28?

00:01:55.230 --> 00:01:57.620
- Those are actually F-5's
that have been painted black

00:01:57.620 --> 00:02:00.230
and they add that little
red star on the tail.

00:02:00.230 --> 00:02:01.800
- [Devin] Aha, interesting.

00:02:01.800 --> 00:02:02.670
- You know, fun fact,

00:02:02.670 --> 00:02:05.300
they actually never identify who the enemy

00:02:05.300 --> 00:02:08.060
or the opposition is in this entire movie.

00:02:08.060 --> 00:02:10.020
And we know for a fact
that it's the same one

00:02:10.020 --> 00:02:11.850
at the beginning and at the end.

00:02:11.850 --> 00:02:14.630
It's just the opposite side, the bad side.

00:02:14.630 --> 00:02:15.780
We don't know.
- Ah.

00:02:15.780 --> 00:02:17.213
- [Pilot] This is Ghostrider 117.

00:02:17.213 --> 00:02:18.680
This bogey's all over me.

00:02:18.680 --> 00:02:19.610
He's got missile locked on me.

00:02:19.610 --> 00:02:20.780
Do I have permission to fire?

00:02:20.780 --> 00:02:23.040
- Do not fire until fired upon.

00:02:23.040 --> 00:02:25.100
- So this is an odd situation.

00:02:25.100 --> 00:02:29.120
It's not unusual for there to
be tension between militaries

00:02:29.120 --> 00:02:31.270
where the governments aren't
exactly friendly to each other.

00:02:31.270 --> 00:02:32.910
I mean, we have to understand this film

00:02:32.910 --> 00:02:35.430
as we are not at war
with this other country.

00:02:35.430 --> 00:02:36.330
This is the Cold War.

00:02:36.330 --> 00:02:37.850
There are tensions.

00:02:37.850 --> 00:02:40.530
But we're not actually in a shooting war.

00:02:40.530 --> 00:02:43.820
The problem is the second
that anyone shoots,

00:02:43.820 --> 00:02:45.350
the other person gets to shoot back.

00:02:45.350 --> 00:02:46.183
In the law of war,

00:02:46.183 --> 00:02:48.750
self-defense is always
available to a party.

00:02:48.750 --> 00:02:51.930
So the pilot here is actually
making the right choice.

00:02:51.930 --> 00:02:53.890
We're not gonna start the war.

00:02:53.890 --> 00:02:56.890
And whoever that commander is, is saying,

00:02:56.890 --> 00:02:59.080
you don't get to shoot
until you're fired upon

00:02:59.080 --> 00:03:01.870
because the US doesn't
wanna start this firing war.

00:03:01.870 --> 00:03:04.470
Yeah and anyone who's in the military,

00:03:04.470 --> 00:03:08.250
who's gonna be going into
either direct combat operations

00:03:08.250 --> 00:03:11.230
or close to combat operations,

00:03:11.230 --> 00:03:12.180
they're gonna be advised

00:03:12.180 --> 00:03:13.660
on all sorts of rules of engagement,

00:03:13.660 --> 00:03:15.000
what they're allowed to do,

00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:17.880
what kind of permission
they have to get to engage.

00:03:17.880 --> 00:03:20.580
But one thing that every
single soldier, marine,

00:03:20.580 --> 00:03:24.010
sailor is taught before
going on a deployment is

00:03:24.010 --> 00:03:26.510
you are allowed to act in self-defense.

00:03:26.510 --> 00:03:28.110
You just have to use your judgment

00:03:28.110 --> 00:03:31.450
and be real careful before
you start a shooting war

00:03:31.450 --> 00:03:33.488
with someone that we're
not in a shooting war with.

00:03:33.488 --> 00:03:36.655
(jet engine whirring)

00:03:38.858 --> 00:03:40.437
- [Goose] Is this your idea of fun, Mav?

00:03:41.400 --> 00:03:43.973
- [Maverick] Greetings. (laughing)

00:03:43.973 --> 00:03:45.550
(upbeat music)

00:03:45.550 --> 00:03:47.660
- [Goose] Watch the birdie.

00:03:47.660 --> 00:03:51.630
- Okay, now I'm just a
humble, country lawyer here.

00:03:51.630 --> 00:03:54.930
But it seems to me that
if an F-14 was inverted

00:03:54.930 --> 00:03:56.540
on top of an F-5,

00:03:56.540 --> 00:04:00.180
that the tail wings would
absolutely smack each other.

00:04:00.180 --> 00:04:04.470
And I mean, even if they weren't, somehow,

00:04:04.470 --> 00:04:07.323
that seems like such an
inherently dangerous thing, that,

00:04:08.490 --> 00:04:11.590
if I was the commanding
officer of these pilots,

00:04:11.590 --> 00:04:13.410
I would not want them to go inverted

00:04:13.410 --> 00:04:16.250
because if they crashed,

00:04:16.250 --> 00:04:19.110
they would've started a
complete international incident.

00:04:19.110 --> 00:04:20.990
That seems like a really bad idea.

00:04:20.990 --> 00:04:22.460
- I mean, this is the kind of behavior,

00:04:22.460 --> 00:04:23.860
which is so Hollywood,

00:04:23.860 --> 00:04:26.437
but they're committing a crime right now.

00:04:26.437 --> 00:04:27.270
(Devin laughing)

00:04:27.270 --> 00:04:28.910
Oddly enough, they're committing a crime.

00:04:28.910 --> 00:04:30.270
As cool as it is,

00:04:30.270 --> 00:04:33.280
the crime is improperly
hazarding an aircraft.

00:04:33.280 --> 00:04:35.280
And the reality of that crime

00:04:35.280 --> 00:04:36.820
is that in time of war,

00:04:36.820 --> 00:04:39.000
you could get the death penalty for that.

00:04:39.000 --> 00:04:39.833
- Yes, sir.

00:04:39.833 --> 00:04:42.580
- But admittedly, looks cool as hell.

00:04:42.580 --> 00:04:44.540
- Well, and that's rule number one.

00:04:44.540 --> 00:04:47.083
Rule number one in the
military is look cool.

00:04:47.950 --> 00:04:49.350
- Trouble
- Come on, man.

00:04:49.350 --> 00:04:50.820
We're get low on gas.

00:04:50.820 --> 00:04:53.221
Let's land this sucker.
(machine beeping)

00:04:53.221 --> 00:04:54.296
(mechanical clanking)

00:04:54.296 --> 00:04:57.463
(jet engine whirring)

00:04:58.888 --> 00:04:59.811
- Goddamn it!

00:04:59.811 --> 00:05:01.030
(Spencer sighs)

00:05:01.030 --> 00:05:05.150
- Okay, so is the instruction
to land, is that an order?

00:05:05.150 --> 00:05:07.836
Did he disobey an order to go up

00:05:07.836 --> 00:05:09.320
and help a fellow pilot?

00:05:09.320 --> 00:05:11.790
Also, I don't know how
you help another pilot

00:05:11.790 --> 00:05:14.180
from another plane, but
that's a separate issue.

00:05:14.180 --> 00:05:15.840
- You know, he's committing

00:05:15.840 --> 00:05:18.330
at least two different crimes right now.

00:05:18.330 --> 00:05:20.550
He's willfully disobeying an order

00:05:20.550 --> 00:05:22.170
from a superior commissioned officer.

00:05:22.170 --> 00:05:24.800
- To land, that plane that's an order!

00:05:24.800 --> 00:05:25.840
- He's instructed the land.

00:05:25.840 --> 00:05:27.230
He's gotta land.

00:05:27.230 --> 00:05:31.400
He's also improperly
hazarding an aircraft.

00:05:31.400 --> 00:05:35.040
The fuel is low and he's
going off to do something,

00:05:35.040 --> 00:05:36.610
apparently give moral support to-

00:05:36.610 --> 00:05:37.970
- [Devin] Yeah, moral support.

00:05:37.970 --> 00:05:41.010
- I don't know if he's planning
on ejecting from his plane,

00:05:41.010 --> 00:05:42.270
into the other plane

00:05:42.270 --> 00:05:43.780
to give maybe a back rub

00:05:43.780 --> 00:05:45.060
and like help out his buddy,

00:05:45.060 --> 00:05:46.980
but either way, it's not looking good.

00:05:46.980 --> 00:05:47.813
- So you're saying,

00:05:47.813 --> 00:05:51.800
it's not standard operating
procedure to fly a plane

00:05:51.800 --> 00:05:56.280
without fuel to try and give
moral support to a pilot

00:05:56.280 --> 00:06:00.900
who seems to be under the
weather for some reason?

00:06:00.900 --> 00:06:02.520
- You know, that's generally frowned upon.

00:06:02.520 --> 00:06:03.766
Yeah.
- Okay.

00:06:03.766 --> 00:06:04.940
("Danger Zone" by Kenny Loggins)

00:06:04.940 --> 00:06:09.390
